Thu Nov 26 07:45:00 UTC 2026 - Jordi Relats <4889953+jordirelats@users.noreply.github.com>

- Update to 0.5.0:
  * Every question that changes the system says what will be done in
    plain words, what it means for you, and shows the exact commands
    underneath as information; Enter never starts a change
  * The queue review has a button per change, shows which change needs
    which, and asks before taking out something others need; after a
    failed step only what was really done leaves the queue
  * A big upgrade asks whether it is worth waiting (size, how new the
    Tumbleweed version is, battery) and can only download it or remind
    you tomorrow from the tray icon
  * Little disk space: the upgrade can run in parts that zypper
    resolves and downloads completely before installing them, after
    freeing the package cache and old kernels
  * Many dependency problems are grouped by cause (a repository not yet
    rebuilt for the new Tumbleweed, packages that no longer exist,
    locks), with wait, leave them as they are, or one by one
  * Failed downloads right after a new Tumbleweed explain that the
    mirrors are still copying it
  * RPMs made for Fedora whose requirements openSUSE has under other
    names get a small bridge package, written without rpm-build, with a
    list of bridges that says when one is no longer needed
  * Repositories that programs leave in /etc/yum.repos.d, which zypper
    ignores, can be added to zypper with their key
  * The tray icon that tells about updates starts at login from the
    package itself (/etc/xdg/autostart), without opening the window
    first; it can be turned off per user in Settings
  * The version is shown in the window title

